<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Menedemus of Eretria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 345–261 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Menedemus of Eretria was a Greek philosopher who founded the Eretrian school, which continued the teaching of Phaedo of Elis and focused on ethics and logic. A citizen of Eretria, he also played a leading part in its politics and served on embassies to the Macedonian kings. He was known for his wit and dialectical skill, though none of his writings survives.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Menedemus belonged to the noble Theopropidai family of Eretria, which held religious authority but had grown poor by his time, and like his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a> he worked as a builder and tent maker. Sent with a military expedition to Megara, probably in 323 or 322 BCE during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamian-war">Lamian War</a>, he went on to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/platonic-academy">Platonic Academy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and resolved to give himself to philosophy. At Megara he formed a lifelong friendship with Asclepiades of Phlius; the two are said to have worked by night so that they could study by day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>With Asclepiades he went to Elis and studied under Anchipylus and Moschus, followers of Phaedo of Elis, and he was also a pupil of Stilpo. He then moved the school to Eretria, after which it took that city's name. Around 310 BCE the two friends stayed at the court of King Nikocreon at Salamis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. Diogenes Laertius tells that Menedemus married the mother of Asclepiades's wife, and on that wife's death left his own wife to his friend and married a richer woman, with whom he had three daughters.</p><p>From about 300 BCE he held a leading place in the politics of Eretria, which had lost much of its old importance. He is said to have worked for its independence and went on many embassies to other Greek cities. He grew close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-ii-gonatas">Antigonus II Gonatas</a>, the king of Macedonia who dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, and this friendship seems to have raised doubts about his loyalty. He took refuge first at the temple of Amphiaraus at Oropus and then with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a>, at whose court he is said to have died of grief; other accounts say he starved himself after failing to persuade the king to free his city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His views survive only in remarks by later writers. Athenaeus quotes Epicrates calling him a Platonist, while other accounts say he preferred Stilpo to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>. Diogenes Laertius reports that he refused to identify the good with the useful and denied the value of negative propositions, holding that only affirmation can express truth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> say he regarded virtue as one, under whatever name, and as intellectual. He counted the poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon">Aratos</a> of Soloi, Lycophron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> and Antagoras of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> among his friends.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Menedemus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rebuilt, it joined the Delian League and later hosted the philosopher <b>Menedemus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/eretria">Eretria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Eretria, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.4,23.8&amp;year=-291" aria-label="Eretria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5142 785 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Menedemus of Eretria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5592 1055h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5592 1055h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.4,23.8&amp;year=-291">What was here in 291 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
